From: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 14:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] nl80211: keep the AP MLD address at a move of the association
 link

nl80211_mlo_assoc_link_move() replaces drv->bssid with the BSSID of the
new association link where drv->bssid is the BSSID of the removed link.
drv->bssid holds one of two addresses:
- For a connection through the connect command, nl80211_parse_mlo_info()
  sets it to the BSSID of the association link.
- With the SME in wpa_supplicant, it is the source address of the
  Association Response frame, which mac80211 translates to the AP MLD
  address.
The check tells the two apart only where the AP MLD address differs from
every link BSSID.

An AP MLD can use the BSSID of one of its links as its MLD address. If
the AP MLD removes that link while it is the association link, drv->bssid
takes the BSSID of another link, and wpas_link_reconfig() copies it into
wpa_s->bssid. In a later scan result, cfg80211 marks the BSS of every
link as associated. For the BSS of a link other than the new association
link, nl80211_check_bss_status() finds that drv->bssid is neither its
BSSID nor the AP MLD address, and deauthenticates.

Replace drv->bssid only where the driver does the SME. With the SME in
wpa_supplicant, drv->bssid stays the AP MLD address.

Signed-off-by: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
---
--- a/src/drivers/driver_nl80211_event.c
+++ b/src/drivers/driver_nl80211_event.c
@@ -377,8 +377,9 @@ static void nl80211_mlo_assoc_link_move(
 		wpa_printf(MSG_DEBUG,
 			   "nl80211: MLO: association link %u removed, use link %d",
 			   mlo->assoc_link_id, i);
-		if (ether_addr_equal(drv->bssid,
-				     mlo->links[mlo->assoc_link_id].bssid))
+		/* With the SME in wpa_supplicant, the BSSID is the AP MLD
+		 * address */
+		if (!(drv->capa.flags & WPA_DRIVER_FLAGS_SME))
 			os_memcpy(drv->bssid, mlo->links[i].bssid, ETH_ALEN);
 		mlo->assoc_link_id = i;
 		return;
